inary.Range(#binary({0..10}), 6, 0)", "#binary({})"}, {32, "Binary.Split", "Splits binary into a list of binaries where the first element of the output list is a binary containing the first pageSize bytes from the source binary, the next element of the output list is a binary containing the next pageSize bytes from the source binary, and so on.", 1, "Binary.Split(Text.ToBinary(""abcde""), 2)", "{#binary({97, 98}), #binary({99, 100}), #binary({101})}"}, {33, "Binary.Split", "Splits binary into a list of binaries where the first element of the output list is a binary containing the first pageSize bytes from the source binary, the next element of the output list is a binary containing the next pageSize bytes from the source binary, and so on.", 2, "Binary.Split(Text.ToBinary(""abc""), 5)", "{#binary({97, 98, 99})}"}, {34, "Binary.Split", "Splits binary into a list of binaries where the first element of the output list is a binary containing the first pageSize bytes from the source binary, the next element of the output list is a binary containing the next pageSize bytes from the source binary, and so on.", 3, "Binary.Split(null, 2)", "error: Expression.Error: We cannot convert the value null to type Binary. | Detail: [Value = null, Type = type binary]"}, {35, "Binary.ToList", "Converts a binary value into a list of numbers.", 1, "Binary.ToList(#binary({0x00, 0x7F, 0xFF}))", "{0, 127, 255}"}, {36, "Binary.ToList", "Converts a binary value into a list of numbers.", 2, "Binary.ToList(#binary({}))", "{}"}, {37, "Binary.ToList", "Converts a binary value into a list of numbers.", 3, "Binary.ToList(null)", "error: Expression.Error: We cannot convert the value null to type Binary. | Detail: [Value = null, Type = type binary]"}, {38, "Binary.ToText", "Returns the result of converting a binary list of numbers binary into a text value. Optionally, encoding may be specified to indicate the encoding to be used in the text value produced The following BinaryEncoding values may be used for encoding. - BinaryEncoding.Base64: Base 64 encoding - BinaryEncoding.Hex: Hex encoding", 1, "Binary.ToText(null)", "null"}, {39, "Binary.ToText", "Returns the result of converting a binary list of numbers binary into a text value. Optionally, encoding may be specified to indicate the encoding to be used in the text value produced The following BinaryEncoding values may be used for encoding. - BinaryEncoding.Base64: Base 64 encoding - BinaryEncoding.Hex: Hex encoding", 2, "Binary.ToText(Binary.FromList({72, 105}))", """SGk="""}, {40, "Binary.ToText", "Returns the result of converting a binary list of numbers binary into a text value. Optionally, encoding may be specified to indicate the encoding to be used in the text value produced The following BinaryEncoding values may be used for encoding. - BinaryEncoding.Base64: Base 64 encoding - BinaryEncoding.Hex: Hex encoding", 3, "Binary.ToText(Binary.FromList({72, 105}), BinaryEncoding.Hex)", """4869"""}, {41, "Binary.View", "Returns a view of binary where the functions specified in handlers are used in lieu of the default behavior of an operation when the operation is applied to the view. If binary is provided, all handler functions are optional. If binary isn't provided, the GetStream handler function is required. If a handler function isn't specified for an operation, the default behavior of the operation is applied to binary instead (except in the case of GetExpression). Handler functions must return a value that is semantically equivalent to the result of applying the operation against binary (or the resulting view in the case of GetExpression). If a handler function raises an error, the default behavior of the operation is applied to the view. Binary.View can be used to implement folding to a data source – the translation of M queries into source-specific operations (for example, to download a section of a file). Refer to the published Power Query custom connector documentation for a more complete description of Binary.View.", 1, "Binary.Length(Binary.View(Text.ToBinary(""hello""), []))", "5"}, {42, "Binary.View", "Returns a view of binary where the functions specified in handlers are used in lieu of the default behavior of an operation when the operation is applied to the view. If binary is provided, all handler functions are optional. If binary isn't provided, the GetStream handler function is required. If a handler function isn't specified for an operation, the default behavior of the operation is applied to binary instead (except in the case of GetExpression). Handler functions must return a value that is semantically equivalent to the result of applying the operation against binary (or the resulting view in the case of GetExpression). If a handler function raises an error, the default behavior of the operation is applied to the view. Binary.View can be used to implement folding to a data source – the translation of M queries into source-specific operations (for example, to download a section of a file). Refer to the published Power Query custom connector documentation for a more complete description of Binary.View.", 2, "Binary.Length(Binary.View(Text.ToBinary(""hello""), [GetLength = () => Number.FromText(""not a number"")]))", "5"}, {43, "Binary.View", "Returns a view of binary where the functions specified in handlers are used in lieu of the default behavior of an operation when the operation is applied to the view. If binary is provided, all handler functions are optional. If binary isn't provided, the GetStream handler function is required. If a handler function isn't specified for an operation, the default behavior of the operation is applied to binary instead (except in the case of GetExpression). Handler functions must return a value that is semantically equivalent to the result of applying the operation against binary (or the resulting view in the case of GetExpression). If a handler function raises an error, the default behavior of the operation is applied to the view. Binary.View can be used to implement folding to a data source – the translation of M queries into source-specific operations (for example, to download a section of a file). Refer to the published Power Query custom connector documentation for a more complete description of Binary.View.", 3, "Text.FromBinary(Binary.View(null, [GetLength = () => 12, GetStream = () => Text.ToBinary(""hello world!"")]))", """hello world!"""}, {44, "Binary.ViewError", "Creates a modified error record from errorRecord which won't trigger a fallback when raised by a handler defined on a view (via Binary.View).", 1, "Binary.ViewError([Reason = ""Expression.Error"", Message = ""boom"", Detail = null])", "[Reason = ""Expression.Error"", Message = ""boom"", Detail = null]"}, {45, "Binary.ViewError", "Creates a modified error record from errorRecord which won't trigger a fallback when raised by a handler defined on a view (via Binary.View).", 2, "Binary.ViewError([Reason = ""Expression.Error"", Message = ""boom""])", "[Reason = ""Expression.Error"", Message = ""boom""]"}, {46, "Binary.ViewError", "Creates a modified error record from errorRecord which won't trigger a fallback when raised by a handler defined on a view (via Binary.View).", 3, "Binary.ViewError(null)", "error: Expression.Error: We cannot convert the value null to type Record. | Detail: [Value = null, Type = type [...]]"}, {47, "Binary.ViewFunction", "Creates a view function based on function that can be handled in a view created by Binary.View. The OnInvoke handler of Binary.View can be used to define a handler for the view function. As with the handlers for built-in operations, if no OnInvoke handler is specified, or if it does not handle the view function, or if an error is raised by the handler, function is applied on top of the view. Refer to the published Power Query custom connector documentation for a more complete description of Binary.View and custom view functions.", 1, "let#(lf)    Size = Binary.ViewFunction((binary as nullable binary) => if binary = null then 0 else Binary.Length(binary))#(lf)in#(lf)    {Size(null), Size(Text.ToBinary(""abcdef""))}", "{0, 6}"}, {48, "Binary.ViewFunction", "Creates a view function based on function that can be handled in a view created by Binary.View. The OnInvoke handler of Binary.View can be used to define a handler for the view function. As with the handlers for built-in operations, if no OnInvoke handler is specified, or if it does not handle the view function, or if an error is raised by the handler, function is applied on top of the view. Refer to the published Power Query custom connector documentation for a more complete description of Binary.View and custom view functions.", 2, "let#(lf)    Size = Binary.ViewFunction((binary as nullable binary) => if binary = null then 0 else Binary.Length(binary)),#(lf)    Viewed = Binary.View(#(lf)        null,#(lf)        [#(lf)            GetStream = () => Text.ToBinary(""abcdef""),#(lf)            OnInvoke = (func, args, index) => 99#(lf)        ]#(lf)    )#(lf)in#(lf)    Size(Viewed)", "99"}, {49, "Binary.ViewFunction", "Creates a view function based on function that can be handled in a view created by Binary.View. The OnInvoke handler of Binary.View can be used to define a handler for the view function. As with the handlers for built-in operations, if no OnInvoke handler is specified, or if it does not handle the view function, or if an error is raised by the handler, function is applied on top of the view. Refer to the published Power Query custom connector documentation for a more complete description of Binary.View and custom view functions.", 3, "let#(lf)    Size = Binary.ViewFunction((binary as nullable binary) => if binary = null then 0 else Binary.Length(binary)),#(lf)    Viewed = Binary.View(#(lf)        null,#(lf)        [#(lf)            GetStream = () => Text.ToBinary(""abcdef""),#(lf)            GetLength = () => 6,#(lf)            OnInvoke = (func, args, index) => error ""not handled""#(lf)        ]#(lf)    )#(lf)in#(lf)    Size(Viewed)", "6"}}),
